# Security trust boundaries — changes to these files require maintainer review.
#
# In a PUBLIC repo the curated dApp allow-list and install links are a phishing
# surface: a look-alike / homoglyph URL merged via a drive-by PR would be
# "blessed" by the wallet's own brand. The Cardano tx / address / key-derivation
# library moves real funds. Any change here must be consciously re-approved.
#
# Two owners, and the second one is not a spare key. `main` requires one
# approving review from a code owner, and GitHub does not let anyone approve
# their own pull request — so with a single owner the rule was unsatisfiable for
# every PR that owner opened, and every merge went through with admin override.
# A rule that is bypassed every time stops being read, which is worse than not
# having it. The second account carries the review role: it opens no PRs here,
# it reviews them, and it is expected to say no. A review round that never
# refuses is a rubber stamp, and a rubber stamp detects nothing.

